 {:class "event_title"} "200 metres Breaststroke, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "6 –  8 August 1928"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/288"} "Zwemstadion, Amsterdam"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/4877"} "21 from 13 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Only triple jumper " [:a {:href "/athletes/72564"} "Mikio Oda"] ", a week before, had ever won Olympic gold for Japan, before " [:a {:href "/athletes/48879"} "Yoshiyuki Tsuruta"] " swam in the 200 metres breaststroke. Tsuruta’s main rival was " [:a {:href "/athletes/54032"} "Erich Rademacher"] ", the German world record holder who missed the " [:a {:href "/results/4774"} "1924 Games"] ", where he would have been a strong favorite."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The two traded the Olympic record in the heats, but Rademacher may have suffered from his participation in the " [:a {:href "/results/38095"} "water polo competition"] " and, although Tsuruta was always pressurized, he was never headed as he won with a metre to spare. " [:a {:href "/athletes/49748"} "Teófilo Yldefonso"] " of the Philippines came perilously close to being knocked out of the competition in his heat, qualified for the semi-finals only as a fastest loser, but swam an inspired final to gain the very first medal for the Philippines in Olympic history."] "\n\n" [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/97368"} "Karl Schäfer"] "  of Austria, who was eliminated at the semi-final stage, had represented his country in the " [:a {:href "/results/12654"} "preceding Winter Olympics"] " as a skater, and would gain greater recognition by winning the men’s figure skating gold at the Winter Games in both " [:a {:href "/results/12712"} "1932"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/12768"} "1936"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R